!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math
!tags: Math Calculus Multivariable

!id: 0xbcsHJFvN
!front: What is \( \nabla \) formally, and in what sense is treating it as a vector a "type pun"?
!back:
\[
  \nabla = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x},\ \frac{\partial}{\partial y},\ \frac{\partial}{\partial z}\right).
\]
It is a "vector" whose components are differential operators, not numbers — so it isn't literally a vector. But treating it as one is productive: combining it with a field via the three familiar vector products (scalar multiplication, dot product, cross product) generates the gradient, divergence, and curl respectively.
~~~

!id: zfFKcMDAhX
!front: What are the domain and codomain field types of \( \operatorname{grad} \), and why is that forced by the product it uses?
!back:
\[
  \operatorname{grad} f = \nabla f : \text{scalar field} \to \text{vector field}.
\]
\( \nabla \) combines with the scalar field \( f \) by scalar multiplication, and scalar \( \times \) vector \( = \) vector, so the result is a vector field.
~~~

!id: 6isoAPsTmG
!front: What are the domain and codomain field types of \( \operatorname{div} \), and why is that forced by the product it uses?
!back:
\[
  \operatorname{div} \mathbf{F} = \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} : \text{vector field} \to \text{scalar field}.
\]
\( \nabla \) combines with the vector field \( \mathbf{F} \) by the dot product, and vector \( \cdot \) vector \( = \) scalar, so the result is a scalar field.
~~~

!id: 6JAaP1RkvV
!front: What are the domain and codomain field types of \( \operatorname{curl} \), and why is that forced by the product it uses?
!back:
\[
  \operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} = \nabla \times \mathbf{F} : \text{vector field} \to \text{vector field}.
\]
\( \nabla \) combines with the vector field \( \mathbf{F} \) by the cross product, and vector \( \times \) vector \( = \) vector, so the result is a vector field.
~~~

!type: Basic Math (and reversed card)

!id: AeU4l1x5nu
!front: \[ \nabla f \]
!back: \[ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x},\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y},\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\right) \]
~~~

!id: HOFZepdukm
!front: \[ \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}, \quad \mathbf{F} = (P, Q, R) \]
!back: \[ \frac{\partial P}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial Q}{\partial y} + \frac{\partial R}{\partial z} \]
~~~

!id: JeLNV4fCiX
!front: \[ \nabla \times \mathbf{F}, \quad \mathbf{F} = (P, Q, R) \]
!back: \[ (R_y - Q_z,\ \ P_z - R_x,\ \ Q_x - P_y) \]
!extra:
This is the formal expansion of the determinant
\[
  \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \partial_x & \partial_y & \partial_z \\ P & Q & R \end{vmatrix},
\]
with subscripts denoting partial derivatives.
~~~

!type: Basic Math

!id: OcyGLv1hiw
!front: What does the divergence of a vector field measure, and what does its sign tell you at a point?
!back: Divergence measures net outflow per unit volume at a point. A positive value means the point behaves like a source (more flux leaving than entering a small neighborhood); a negative value means it behaves like a sink.
~~~

!id: 4S9UZ9cRbx
!front: What does the curl of a vector field measure, and what does its direction represent?
!back: Curl measures the local rotation of the field. Its direction is the axis about which the field locally rotates (by the right-hand rule), and its magnitude reflects the strength of that rotation.
~~~

!id: gCc1H7Drsb
!front: Why is curl, as usually written, specific to \( \mathbb{R}^3 \), while gradient and divergence make sense in any \( \mathbb{R}^n \)?
!back: Gradient and divergence are built only from partial derivatives and a dot product, both of which make sense in any dimension. Curl is built from a cross product, \( \nabla \times \mathbf{F} \), and the cross product as an operation returning a vector in the same space is specific to \( \mathbb{R}^3 \).
!extra: In \( \mathbb{R}^2 \) one instead uses the scalar curl \( Q_x - P_y \), the \( \mathbf{k} \)-component of the \( \mathbb{R}^3 \) curl when \( \mathbf{F}=(P,Q,0) \) doesn't depend on \( z \). This is exactly the integrand appearing in Green's theorem.
~~~
